VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: R12
ATTEMPT: 2
---
Scope: internal/services/accounts/projection.go, projection_test.go,
internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/r12_default_asof_recurring_test.go
(.swarm/manifests/R12.2.files). Contract: .swarm/briefs/R12.md "Attempt 2".

INTEGRITY: fix is present and applied symmetrically (asOfUTC := asOf.UTC();
occ := dayOf(rp.NextExpected.UTC()); both feed keyOf/dayOf consistently).
`go build ./...`, `go vet ./...` clean; `go test -race -count=1 ./...`
RC=0, zero FAIL lines across the whole module. All committed probes were
created and removed from the tree during this review; `git status --porcelain`
confirms no residue (only R12's own declared files remain modified/untracked).

REQUIRED EVIDENCE (yearly/monthly/negative-offset tests): all three pass
under TZ=UTC, America/New_York, Asia/Tokyo, Pacific/Midway with -count=1.
Independently verified.

"Is UTC necessary" claim: verified TRUE. Mutating the fix to normalise into
asOf's own Location instead of UTC (on a scratch copy outside the repo)
reproduces the drop: both TestProject_YearlyOccurrenceOneHourAfter... and
...MonthlyCrossingIsNearDate... FAIL against that mutant. The worker's
rejection of asOf-Location normalisation is correct.

Mutation checks: normalising asOfDay to UTC but leaving occ un-normalised is
caught by the committed tests (2 FAIL). Normalising occ but not asOfDay is a
no-op given the codebase's existing UTC-only convention for NextExpected, so
it proves nothing either way.

"Exact instant" / "one second after" claims: verified true and consistent
with the disclosed day-granularity design (an occurrence 1s after asOf but
on the same UTC calendar day is excluded, same as an exact-instant match).
Not a hidden defect; this matches the doc comment's own stated behaviour.

PRIME TARGET B (rebuilt MCP test): reran the ORIGINAL (attempt-1-style,
Local-`now`-anchored) fixture and expectation against attempt 2's code
across five ambient TZs (UTC, America/New_York, Asia/Tokyo, Pacific/Midway,
Pacific/Kiritimati). It still passes in every zone — the fixture-generation
offset and the grid's UTC offset cancel because both the CSV dates and asOf
are derived from the same `now`. No evidence the rewrite is hiding a
regression; not a basis for FAIL on its own.

PRIME TARGET A — CONFIRMED, AND WORSE THAN THE WORKER'S OWN DISCLOSURE:

The worker's report says BalanceAt's cutoff (dayOf(asOf) in asOf's own
Location, via `start`) and Project's grid cutoff (now dayOf(asOf.UTC()))
"can differ by up to a day when asOf isn't UTC, that this predates the fix,
and is unobservable given day-granularity output." Both halves of that claim
are false:

1. NOT unobservable. Built a fixture (Asia/Tokyo, asOf = 2026-09-01 20:00
   JST = 2026-09-01 11:00 UTC — a LATE local time-of-day) with one real
   transaction dated 2026-09-01 05:00 UTC, Amount -300, which genuinely
   happened 6 hours before asOf's instant. Calling BalanceAt directly
   returns Amount=1000.00 (the -300 tx is dropped: its UTC calendar day is
   `After` BalanceAt's Local-Location cutoff). Calling Project with the same
   fixture returns Minimum=1000.00, Crossing=zero, SuggestedTopUp=0.00 —
   even though the true balance is 700.00, below the 800.00 threshold. This
   is a real dollar-value discrepancy in exactly the fields the brief names
   (Minimum, Crossing, SuggestedTopUp), not a cosmetic label difference
   invisible at "day granularity." (Probe run and deleted; git status
   confirmed clean before/after.)

2. NOT pre-existing. Before attempt 2, Project computed `asOfDay :=
   dayOf(asOf)` — literally the same call, on the same input, in the same
   Location, as BalanceAt's `atDay := dayOf(at)` (at == asOf). The two
   cutoffs were therefore ALWAYS IDENTICAL prior to this change; there was
   no gap to be "pre-existing." Attempt 2 introduces the gap by moving only
   Project's cutoff to `dayOf(asOf.UTC())` while leaving BalanceAt (called
   internally via `start`) on `dayOf(asOf)`. Confirmed by re-deriving the
   pre-attempt-2 diff: `asOfDay := dayOf(asOf)` (old) vs `asOfDay :=
   dayOf(asOfUTC)` (new); BalanceAt's own call is untouched.

Mechanism, generalised: for a positive-UTC-offset zone, BalanceAt's
effective cutoff (as a UTC calendar day) is always asOf's local calendar day
minus one. Project's UTC-anchored asOfDay is asOf's local calendar day minus
one ONLY when asOf's local time-of-day is early enough that asOf.UTC()
hasn't rolled onto the next UTC day; once local time-of-day passes the
offset threshold (e.g. after 09:00 in JST, UTC+9 — the ordinary case for a
daytime query), asOf.UTC()'s calendar day advances by one and Project's
asOfDay becomes asOf's local day itself. That day is then excluded from
BOTH BalanceAt's sum (cutoff didn't reach it) AND Project's walk (it's
`asOfDay`, deliberately excluded as "already reflected in the starting
balance" — except it isn't). Any real, already-posted transaction dated
that UTC calendar day is silently dropped from the entire computation. The
same asymmetric gap recurs for negative-offset zones once local
time-of-day + |offset| crosses a UTC day boundary. This fires for the
*ordinary* daytime case in non-UTC zones, not a rare edge — every
`get_balance_projection` call with no `as_of` from an APAC host made after
local mid-morning is exposed.

This is a real, newly-introduced, dollar-observable regression in the
starting-balance/Minimum/Crossing/SuggestedTopUp path, misdiagnosed in the
worker's own report as harmless and pre-existing. No committed test in
R12.2.files exercises it (all committed tests use asOf built at a fixed
early local hour — 08:00 JST / 18:00 Midway — which lands in the "no gap"
branch of the mechanism above); the gap survives `go test -race ./...`
clean.

The claim under test — "an occurrence strictly after asOf is now always
written to a label the balance walk visits, in every timezone, and nothing
else changed" — is false on the "nothing else changed" clause: the
BalanceAt/Project cutoff relationship changed, and it now drops real,
already-happened transactions in a materially observable way.

FINDINGS SUMMARY
- FAIL: Prime Target A confirmed as a genuine, newly-introduced,
  dollar-observable defect (not pre-existing, not unobservable). Affects
  Minimum/Crossing/SuggestedTopUp for real transactions landing in the gap
  day, on ordinary (non-early-morning) queries in non-UTC zones.
- Prime Target B: no defect found; original and rebuilt test expectations
  agree across 5 TZs for this fixture shape.
- UTC-vs-asOf-Location necessity: worker's claim verified correct.
- Required TZ sweep (yearly/monthly/negative-offset): all pass, 4 TZs.
- Mutation checks: one meaningful mutation caught by committed tests; the
  other is a no-op given the codebase's UTC-only NextExpected convention.
- go build / go vet / go test -race ./...: all clean.
